From: mscharged-port
Subject: Decode native Wii data read by original match-loading consumers
Upstream-base: f75ccf1ad20e5bd7585962990db08067c0ae498a

E13 native data transport for Matching units that read big-endian Wii data
directly during match loading. On the Wii these are plain big-endian loads; on
the host the bytes keep their authored order, so host loads returned
byte-swapped values. Original chunk walks, allocations, copy loops, strides,
offsets, conversions, retail quirks and control flow are unchanged; only the
native branches decode the data.

- Game/NetMeshModelLoader.cpp, Game/NetMeshEdge.cpp (goal-net physics mesh):
  vertex indices are read back from each packet's GX display list, which native
  builds keep big-endian (glxDisplayList.cpp), and positions, normals and texture
  coordinates are read from the model's GPU vertex streams, which keep their Wii
  byte order. Swapped indices made the vertex lookups fail and the loader
  dereferenced uninitialized vertex pointers in CreateStaticPhysicsPrimitives.
  The native branches decode the index cells and read the numeric twin of each
  completed stream (platform ReadRLGNumericStream). The retail stitched-stride
  arithmetic is preserved.
- Game/Physics/CharacterPhysicsElement.cpp: the element count and the 0xA0
  CharacterPhysicsElement records are Wii big-endian words. The swapped count
  produced an invalid nlMalloc request in
  CharacterLoader::FinalizeLoadingCharacterPhysicsElements. The native branches
  read the count and expand each record through platform
  ReadCharacterPhysicsWord/ReadCharacterPhysicsElement (names stay byte strings).
- Game/Drawable/DrawableCharacter.cpp, Game/Render/SkinAnimatedNPC.cpp: the
  per-character and stadium-NPC shadowLevel colour is packed with a host-order
  word cast; the original shadow-lighting stage (ColourFromWiiWord) reads a
  numeric Wii RGBA word, so on little-endian hosts red collapsed to ~1/255 and
  characters rendered teal/green in stadiums. The native branches pack with the
  existing ColourToWiiWord, as GameObjectLighting's impostor producer does.

